/* GENERATED by knowledge/scripts/build-course-notes.mjs on 2026-10-26: Evelyn's answers, the general layer and her shelf, per lesson. Don't edit; rebuild. */
window.SCHOOL_NOTES = {"1-1":{"evelyn":[],"general":["Skin renews itself in roughly four weeks when young, slower with age (five to six weeks or more). A product or treatment that works through turnover (acids, retinoids, acne routines, pigment correction) needs at least one full cycle to show, and usually two or three. That's why consistent home care over 8 to 12 weeks matters more than any single visit, and why a routine shouldn't be judged in a week.","Deeper skin tones (more melanin) are more likely to leave a dark mark after any irritation, breakout or over-aggressive treatment (post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation). Gentler acids such as mandelic and lactic, careful peel depth, no picking, and daily SPF matter even more. Lighter skin tends to go red rather than dark. Neither is \"better\"; the aftercare rules just weigh differently.","Pore size is set by genetics and oil flow. Pores can't be closed or shrunk, but they look smaller when they're kept clear of oil and dead skin (exfoliation, retinoids, clay or sulfur masks) and when the skin around them is firmer. Nothing \"tightens\" a pore for good."],"shelf":[]},"1-2":{"evelyn":["What do you ask a client first: What are your skin concerns and what does your current routine look like?","Morning: what do you recommend from your shelf: For morning routine with dry/normal skin clients look like barrier cream cleanser, hydraremedy gel serum, soothing radiance toner, 5%-8% (depends on clients tolerance), cran peptide, eye gel and mineral SPF or brilliant defense For oily/combo/normal it usually looks like ultra gentle gel cleanser, moisture balance, eye gel, 8-15% mandelic (depends on tolerance), barrier gel cream or mineral SPF or brilliant Sometimes its mixed around really varies","Her picks: Barrier Balance Creamy Cleanser, Soothing Radiance Toner, 8% L-Mandelic Serum, 5% L-Mandelic Serum, 15% L-Mandelic Serum, 11% L-Mandelic Serum, Ultra Gentle Gel Cleanser, HydraRemedy Gel Serum and 8 more."],"general":["Type is what the skin tends to be: oily, dry, combination (oily T-zone, drier cheeks) or normal. It's mostly genetic and changes slowly, with age and hormones.","Condition is what the skin is doing right now: dehydrated, sensitized, congested, breaking out, hyperpigmented. Conditions come and go, and products, weather, stress and habits move them.","Any type can have any condition. Oily skin can be dehydrated; dry skin can break out.","Dry skin makes less oil. It feels rough or flaky most of the time and usually wants richer moisturizers.","Dehydrated skin is short on water, not oil. It feels tight after cleansing, looks dull, shows fine lines that smooth out with hydration, and can be oily at the same time. Over-cleansing, over-exfoliating, heat, wind and dry air are common causes. Hydrating toners, humectants (glycerin, hyaluronic acid) and a moisturizer that seals them in usually help.","Pore size is set by genetics and oil flow. Pores can't be closed or shrunk, but they look smaller when they're kept clear of oil and dead skin (exfoliation, retinoids, clay or sulfur masks) and when the skin around them is firmer. Nothing \"tightens\" a pore for good."],"shelf":[]},"1-3":{"evelyn":["When should a client stop a product and message you: If anything is burning, stinging or feels sensitive, reach out to me right away so we can make changes. If your skin has eczema, dermatitis, psoriasis or any unusual reaction or flare-up, stop your actives immediately and message me.","What should they avoid: Do not over exfoliate avoid using actives without being given aftercare instructions first, always follow aftercare instructions and if anything is burning, stinging or sensitive reach out immediately to make changes. If skin is experiencing Eczema,dermatitis, Psoriasis or any unusual reactions/flare ups stop actives immediately (list examples of active ingredients for them so they are aware of what to stop)"],"general":["The outermost layer of skin, with its oils (lipids), keeps water in and irritants out. When it's worn down (too many actives, harsh cleansers, hot water, scrubbing, cold wind, sunburn), skin stings with products that used to be fine, looks red or shiny-tight, flakes, and breaks out more. A barrier recovers with fewer actives, a gentle cleanser, a moisturizer with ceramides or other barrier lipids, and SPF. Most \"my skin suddenly hates everything\" stories are a tired barrier.","Dry skin makes less oil. It feels rough or flaky most of the time and usually wants richer moisturizers.","Dehydrated skin is short on water, not oil. It feels tight after cleansing, looks dull, shows fine lines that smooth out with hydration, and can be oily at the same time. Over-cleansing, over-exfoliating, heat, wind and dry air are common causes. Hydrating toners, humectants (glycerin, hyaluronic acid) and a moisturizer that seals them in usually help.","A full routine isn't required to start. Cleanser, moisturizer and SPF are the base; one treatment product aimed at the main concern is the first addition. Adding everything at once makes a reaction impossible to trace.","Humectants (glycerin, hyaluronic acid, sodium PCA, aloe) pull water into the skin. They work best on damp skin with a moisturizer over them; on their own in dry air they can leave skin feeling tighter.","Emollients and occlusives (squalane, shea, oils, dimethicone, petrolatum) smooth and seal. Gel creams are lighter, for oily or acne-prone skin; creams and balms for dry.","Ceramides, cholesterol and fatty acids are the skin's own barrier lipids; good when skin is sensitized or over-exfoliated.","Niacinamide (vitamin B3) calms, helps oil balance and the barrier, and softens dark marks over time. Generally well tolerated and fine alongside most actives, including vitamin C (the \"don't mix them\" idea is outdated)."],"shelf":[{"id":"fr-barrier-gel-cream","name":"Barrier Care Gel Cream","brand":"Face Reality","price":38,"about":"Repairs, moisturizes and comforts, with Multi-Restore Complex. Acne-safe."},{"id":"fr-cran-peptide-cream","name":"Cran-Peptide Cream","brand":"Face Reality","price":33,"about":"Rich and restorative. Acne-safe."},{"id":"fr-hydrabalance-gel","name":"HydraBalance Gel","brand":"Face Reality","price":34,"about":"Hydrates and balances oil. Acne-safe."},{"id":"gm-barrier-repair-cream","name":"Barrier Repair Cream","brand":"GlyMed Plus","price":83.2,"about":"With amino acids. Part of the GlyMed Plus Elite line; step 3 of its routine: balance."},{"id":"gm-cell-protection-balm","name":"Cell Protection Balm","brand":"GlyMed Plus","price":34,"about":"Step 3 of the GlyMed Plus routine: balance."}]},"2-1":{"evelyn":["The order of steps, morning and night: Morning: cleanser, toner, serum, eye gel, moisturizer, SPF. Night: cleanse (double cleanse if you wore makeup or SPF), toner, your treatment product (your active or acne med), serum, eye gel, moisturizer. Thin to thick, and a spot treatment goes on last, only on the spot. Please be sure to wash hands before applying eye products to prevent actives from bleeding into eye area and causing dryness or irritation.","When can I wash my face, and with what: Leave it alone until tonight or tomorrow morning, then a gentle cleanser with lukewarm water, fingertips only. Moisturize and SPF in the morning. No scrubs, acids or exfoliating cleansers for a few days."],"general":["Gel and foaming cleansers lift oil well; usually for oily and combination skin, or a second cleanse.","Cream and milk cleansers are gentler; usually for dry, dehydrated or sensitized skin.","Cleansing oils and balms dissolve makeup and SPF; often the first step of a double cleanse at night.","Cleansing twice a day is enough. \"Squeaky clean\" usually means stripped."],"shelf":[{"id":"fr-barrier-cream-cleanser","name":"Barrier Balance Creamy Cleanser","brand":"Face Reality","price":37,"about":"A daily calming wash. Acne-safe."},{"id":"fr-gentle-gel-cleanser","name":"Ultra Gentle Gel Cleanser (travel size)","brand":"Face Reality","price":14,"about":"A daily antioxidant wash. Acne-safe."},{"id":"fr-mandelic-face-and-body","name":"L-Mandelic Face and Body Wash","brand":"Face Reality","price":38,"about":"Cleanses and brightens. Acne-safe."},{"id":"fr-ultra-gentle-cleanser","name":"Ultra Gentle Gel Cleanser","brand":"Face Reality","price":33,"about":"A daily antioxidant wash. Acne-safe."},{"id":"gm-glycolic-cleanser","name":"Glycolic Facial Cleanser","brand":"GlyMed Plus","price":41,"about":"With 10% glycolic acid. Step 1 of the GlyMed Plus routine: cleanse."},{"id":"gm-vitamin-c-cleanser","name":"Vitamin C Cleanser","brand":"GlyMed Plus","price":47,"about":"Step 1 of the GlyMed Plus routine: cleanse."}]},"2-2":{"evelyn":["Do I need everything you recommended, or what should I start with: If you're working with a budget, we can absolutely start with the essentials: cleanser, hydrator, moisturizer, and SPF**, then gradually introduce additional products as you're ready. Just keep in mind that this approach may slow down your progress since we won't be addressing all of your skin concerns right away. However, if you're enrolled in my Acne Program, I highly recommend starting with your complete recommended routine, including your treatment products (actives). These play an important role in targeting acne, preventing new breakouts, and helping us achieve consistent results. My goal is always to create a routine that's both effective and realistic for you, so we can discuss your priorities and budget together!"],"general":["Humectants (glycerin, hyaluronic acid, sodium PCA, aloe) pull water into the skin. They work best on damp skin with a moisturizer over them; on their own in dry air they can leave skin feeling tighter.","Emollients and occlusives (squalane, shea, oils, dimethicone, petrolatum) smooth and seal. Gel creams are lighter, for oily or acne-prone skin; creams and balms for dry.","Ceramides, cholesterol and fatty acids are the skin's own barrier lipids; good when skin is sensitized or over-exfoliated.","Niacinamide (vitamin B3) calms, helps oil balance and the barrier, and softens dark marks over time. Generally well tolerated and fine alongside most actives, including vitamin C (the \"don't mix them\" idea is outdated).","Peptides signal the skin to make more collagen and support firmness. Gentle; usually fine with everything."],"shelf":[{"id":"fr-barrier-gel-cream","name":"Barrier Care Gel Cream","brand":"Face Reality","price":38,"about":"Repairs, moisturizes and comforts, with Multi-Restore Complex. Acne-safe."},{"id":"fr-cran-peptide-cream","name":"Cran-Peptide Cream","brand":"Face Reality","price":33,"about":"Rich and restorative. Acne-safe."},{"id":"fr-hydrabalance-gel","name":"HydraBalance Gel","brand":"Face Reality","price":34,"about":"Hydrates and balances oil. Acne-safe."},{"id":"gm-barrier-repair-cream","name":"Barrier Repair Cream","brand":"GlyMed Plus","price":83.2,"about":"With amino acids. Part of the GlyMed Plus Elite line; step 3 of its routine: balance."},{"id":"gm-cell-protection-balm","name":"Cell Protection Balm","brand":"GlyMed Plus","price":34,"about":"Step 3 of the GlyMed Plus routine: balance."}]},"2-3":{"evelyn":["Which sunscreen should I use: Broad spectrum, SPF 30 or higher, every morning, enough of it (about 2 thin fingers worth), reapplied if you're outside or in a room with windows. Mineral if your skin is sensitive, acne-prone or freshly treated. The ones I carry are the ones I trust; ask me which fits you.","Anything you always say about sunscreen: Every morning, rain or shine, SPF 30 or higher, enough of it, and reapplied when you're outside. It's the one product that protects everything else we're doing: every active makes you burn more easily, and sun is what brings dark spots back.","Habits and everyday tips you give: SPF every single morning and reapplied every 2-3 hours outside, a hat in strong sun, hands off breakouts, and patience: marks take months to fade, faster on skin that isn't being irritated."],"general":["Mineral (zinc oxide, titanium dioxide) sits on the skin and reflects and absorbs UV; usually the first choice for sensitive, acne-prone, post-treatment and pregnant clients. Can leave a cast on deeper tones; tinted versions help.","Chemical (avobenzone, octocrylene and others) absorb UV; lighter textures, more likely to sting after a treatment.","Either way: broad spectrum, SPF 30 or more, enough of it (about a quarter teaspoon for the face), reapplied every two hours in the sun. Makeup with SPF is a top-up, not the base.","UV is the main cause of early lines, uneven tone, dark spots and melasma, and it undoes pigment work. Daily broad-spectrum SPF 30 or higher is the one thing every esthetician agrees on. After any exfoliating treatment or active, skin burns more easily for days to weeks."],"shelf":[{"id":"fr-brilliant-defense-50","name":"Brilliant Defense SPF 50","brand":"Face Reality","price":42,"about":"Broad-spectrum SPF 50 sunscreen, with BrightShield Complex. Acne-safe."},{"id":"fr-daily-spf-30","name":"Daily SPF 30 Plus","brand":"Face Reality","price":36,"about":"Broad-spectrum SPF 30 sunscreen. Acne-safe."},{"id":"fr-mineral-matte-spf-28","name":"Mineral Matte SPF 28","brand":"Face Reality","price":36,"about":"Broad-spectrum SPF 28 sunscreen. Acne-safe."},{"id":"fr-powdermineral-spf-50","name":"PowerMineral SPF 50","brand":"Face Reality","price":42,"about":"Broad-spectrum SPF 50 sunscreen, with CalmShield Complex. Acne-safe."},{"id":"mx-spf-50-mineral-spf","name":"Mineral SPF 50","brand":"Mixi Skincare","price":65,"about":"UVA/UVB protection. Medical-grade sunscreen."}]},"3-1":{"evelyn":[],"general":["Common triggers for stinging and redness on sensitized skin. \"Unscented\" can still contain masking fragrance; \"fragrance-free\" is the clearer label.","Vitamin C (ascorbic acid and its derivatives) is an antioxidant that brightens, supports collagen and helps SPF do its job; used in the morning under sunscreen. Pure ascorbic acid can sting on a sensitized barrier.","Niacinamide, azelaic acid, tranexamic acid, kojic acid, licorice, arbutin, alpha-arbutin slow pigment production; they work gradually (8 to 12 weeks) and only hold with daily SPF.","Hydroquinone is a prescription-strength lightener in many places and a doctor's call."],"shelf":[]},"3-2":{"evelyn":["Do I need everything you recommended, or what should I start with: If you're working with a budget, we can absolutely start with the essentials: cleanser, hydrator, moisturizer, and SPF**, then gradually introduce additional products as you're ready. Just keep in mind that this approach may slow down your progress since we won't be addressing all of your skin concerns right away. However, if you're enrolled in my Acne Program, I highly recommend starting with your complete recommended routine, including your treatment products (actives). These play an important role in targeting acne, preventing new breakouts, and helping us achieve consistent results. My goal is always to create a routine that's both effective and realistic for you, so we can discuss your priorities and budget together!","Can I keep using my own products with the ones you gave me: Usually yes; what matters is not doubling up: two exfoliants, two retinoids or several actives at once. Send me a photo of what you're using and I'll tell you what stays, what goes and what waits.","The order of steps, morning and night: Morning: cleanser, toner, serum, eye gel, moisturizer, SPF. Night: cleanse (double cleanse if you wore makeup or SPF), toner, your treatment product (your active or acne med), serum, eye gel, moisturizer. Thin to thick, and a spot treatment goes on last, only on the spot. Please be sure to wash hands before applying eye products to prevent actives from bleeding into eye area and causing dryness or irritation."],"general":["A full routine isn't required to start. Cleanser, moisturizer and SPF are the base; one treatment product aimed at the main concern is the first addition. Adding everything at once makes a reaction impossible to trace.","Products from different lines can usually sit in the same routine. What matters is the ingredient overlap: two exfoliants, two retinoids or several actives at once are the problem, not the brand mix. Whether a particular client should keep a particular product is a question for Evelyn.","A pea for a retinoid or acne medication across the whole face; a few drops of serum; a nickel of moisturizer; a quarter teaspoon of sunscreen for the face alone. More active isn't faster, it's just more irritating."],"shelf":[]},"3-3":{"evelyn":["I skipped my routine for a few days. Do I start over: No, a few days doesn't undo anything. Pick it back up at the same strength. If it's been a week or more, go back to a few nights a week with your actives first, then build up again. Reach out if you're unsure.","What can I do between visits to keep my results: Stick to the routine, SPF every day, hands off your face, clean pillowcases and phone screen, and don't start new products without checking with me. That's most of the result right there.","Habits and everyday tips you give: Habits & Everyday Skincare Tips — At Lumevina Aesthetics Spa, I educate clients on how their everyday habits can affect their skin, especially when treating acne, sensitivity, dehydration, and a compromised skin barrier. My approach focuses on consistency, proper hygiene, realistic lifestyle changes, and maintaining a personalized skincare routine. 1. Pillowcases & Bedding - Change pillowcases regularly, ideally 2–3 times per week, especially if you're acne-prone or use heavy hair products. - Keep bedding clean to minimize the buildup of oils, sweat, and product residue. - Avoid heavily fragranced detergents and fabric softeners if your skin is sensitive or easily irritated. - Clean towels should always be used when drying your face. Gently pat rather than rub. ### 8. Sunscreen & Sun Exposure - Daily SPF is one of my biggest non-negotiables. - Apply a generous amount of broad-spectrum SPF 30 or higher every morning. I generally recommend approximately two finger-lengths for the face and neck, adjusting as needed for adequate coverage. - Reapply every two hours while outdoors, and after swimming or sweating. - Sun protection is especially important for clients treating acne marks, hyperpigmentation, or undergoing chemical peels. - I remind clients that sunscreen is important even when it's cloudy. - Clients undergoing microchanneling or certain advanced treatments must follow their specific aftercare instructions, which may temporarily restrict sunscreen application immediately after treatment. ### 9. Skincare Consistency & Skin Barrier Health - Consistency is more important than using an excessive number of products. - Follow the personalized morning and evening routine provided by your esthetician. - Do not introduce multiple new active ingredients at once without guidance. - More exfoliation does not mean faster results. - Dryness, burning, persistent redness, and excessive peeling may indicate irritation or an impaired skin barrier. - If irritation develops, contact me before continuing or increasing active ingredients. - Barrier repair days may be incorporated into acne routines to allow the skin to recover. - Acne treatments such as mandelic acid and benzoyl peroxide must be introduced and adjusted according to skin tolerance. - Apply skincare in the recommended order, allowing products to absorb and dry as directed, particularly before applying stronger acne treatments. - Avoid applying strong actives around the eyes, eyelids, corners of the nose, and lips unless specifically instructed. - Never assume that an active breakout means your routine isn't working. Acne treatment requires time, consistency, and periodic adjustments. ### 10. Expectations & Professional Guidance - Healthy skin is a long-term commitment, not an overnight transformation. - Acne treatment often requires several months of consistency, depending on the type and severity of acne. - Professional treatments and homecare work together. One cannot completely replace the other. - Avoid constantly switching products based on social media trends. - Products that work for someone else may not be appropriate for your skin. - I encourage clients to communicate any changes, irritation, new medications, or concerns so their treatment plan can be adjusted safely. - My philosophy is to educate clients rather than overwhelm them with unnecessary products or unrealistic expectations. Important chatbot guideline: These are general educational recommendations, not universal rules. The AI should never assume every client needs the same products, diet changes, or routine. It should prioritize the client's personalized Lumevina Aesthetics Spa treatment plan and recommend contacting me directly for individualized advice, particularly regarding active ingredients, irritation, or post-treatment care.","Her picks: Phones & Touching Your Face, Regularly clean your phone screen, especially if it frequently touches your cheeks., Avoid touching your face unnecessarily or resting your face in your hands., Never pick, squeeze, or pop active breakouts. This increases the risk of inflammation, post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation, and scarring., Keep your hands clean before applying skincare products., Hair Products & Hair Hygiene, Be mindful of hair products containing heavy oils, waxes, and pomades, especially if you experience forehead, temple, or hairline breakouts., Keep hair away from your face when sleeping or exercising if products tend to transfer onto your skin. and 27 more.","I'm traveling. What should I bring: Cleanser, moisturizer, SPF and your main treatment product; leave the rest. Plane air is dry, so bring a hydrating mist or go heavier on moisturizer, and if you'll be in the sun, ease off the actives that week.","How long does a bottle usually last: Roughly: a 1 oz serum, 2 to 3 months; a 2 oz moisturizer, 2 to 3 months; a 6 oz cleanser, 3 to 4 months; a 2 oz sunscreen used properly on the face, about a month. If something's lasting much longer, you may be using too little."],"general":["A few skipped days don't reset progress. Pick the routine back up at the same strength; a week or more away from a retinoid or acid, go back to a few nights a week first.","Cleanser, moisturizer, sunscreen and the one treatment product the routine is built around; leave the rest. Plane air is dry, so a hydrating mist and more moisturizer help, and sun exposure on vacation is the usual reason to go easier on actives for a week.","A 1 oz serum used once a day lasts about two to three months; a 2 oz moisturizer about two to three months; a 6 oz cleanser three to four months; a 2 oz sunscreen used properly on the face, about a month. Opened products are best used within six to twelve months; sunscreen and vitamin C lose strength faster when they're warm or in light."],"shelf":[]},"4-1":{"evelyn":["Starting the mandelic serums: Start with the strength I give you, usually the lowest, a few nights a week, and build up to nightly as your skin allows. Moving up a strength is my call, after a few weeks with no stinging or flaking. If it ever burns or your skin gets sensitive, back off and message me."],"general":["AHAs (alpha hydroxy acids) loosen dead cells on the surface and, over time, help texture, tone and dullness. Glycolic is the smallest and strongest; lactic is gentler and hydrating; mandelic is the largest and gentlest, works more slowly, and is a common choice for sensitive skin, deeper skin tones and acne because it's less likely to cause irritation and dark marks, and it has some antibacterial action.","BHA (salicylic acid) is oil-soluble, so it gets into pores: blackheads, congestion, oily skin. Usually 0.5 to 2% at home.","Enzymes (papaya, pumpkin, pineapple) digest dead surface cells without acid; the gentlest option, common in facials.","Scrubs (physical exfoliants) can work on body skin; on the face, harsh grains are a common cause of irritation and are usually skipped when acids or retinoids are in the routine.","Too much exfoliation looks like: shine with tightness, redness, stinging, more breakouts, flaking. Less is more; most skin does well with acids a few nights a week, not every night."],"shelf":[{"id":"fr-11pct-mandelic-serum","name":"11% L-Mandelic Serum","brand":"Face Reality","price":46,"about":"Exfoliates and brightens."},{"id":"fr-15pct-mandelic-serum","name":"15% L-Mandelic Serum","brand":"Face Reality","price":48,"about":"Exfoliates and brightens."},{"id":"fr-5pct-mandelic-serum","name":"5% L-Mandelic Serum","brand":"Face Reality","price":42,"about":"Exfoliates and brightens."},{"id":"fr-8pct-mandelic-serum","name":"8% L-Mandelic Serum","brand":"Face Reality","price":44,"about":"Exfoliates and brightens."},{"id":"fr-glowtone-corrective","name":"GlowTone Corrective Serum","brand":"Face Reality","price":64,"about":"Targets discoloration, with Hexa-Bright Complex."},{"id":"fr-hydraremedy-gel-serum","name":"HydraRemedy Gel Serum","brand":"Face Reality","price":46,"about":"Hydrates, repairs and soothes, with Tri-Recovery Complex. Acne-safe."}]},"4-2":{"evelyn":["Starting the mandelic serums: Start with the strength I give you, usually the lowest, a few nights a week, and build up to nightly as your skin allows. Moving up a strength is my call, after a few weeks with no stinging or flaking. If it ever burns or your skin gets sensitive, back off and message me.","What not to mix, or not on the same night: Not on the same night: a retinoid with an acid; two exfoliants; benzoyl peroxide with retinol. Vitamin C and Mandelic Acid goes in the morning, actives at night. Nothing active on skin that's freshly treated or waxed. Niacinamide and vitamin C are fine together.","When should a client stop a product and message you: If anything is burning, stinging or feels sensitive, reach out to me right away so we can make changes. If your skin has eczema, dermatitis, psoriasis or any unusual reaction or flare-up, stop your actives immediately and message me."],"general":["One new product at a time, two weeks apart, so a reaction has a clear cause.","Patch test behind the ear or on the jaw for two or three nights before using an active on the whole face.","Actives start two or three nights a week and build up as the skin allows. A new sting or flaking means slow down, not push through.","Expect the first few weeks of a retinoid or acid to include some dryness or small breakouts; judge a routine at 8 to 12 weeks, not 8 to 12 days."],"shelf":[]},"4-3":{"evelyn":["What happens, step by step: Clean, dry skin, then a sterile blade glides over the face at an angle, lifting dead surface cells and the fine vellus hair. A few minutes per area, then calming products and SPF, or straight into your facial or peel.","Does it hurt? How does it feel: Painless; a light scraping sensation, like a soft brush. Most people find it relaxing.","How they'll look after, and for how long: None. A little pink for a few hours, smooth right away, makeup glides on from the next day. SPF, hydration and proper moisturizer matters more than usual for a few days.","How often should they book it: Every 4 to 6 weeks As the fine hair returns, usually 3 to 5 weeks.","When will they see results, and how long do they last: Instantly smoother, brighter skin and makeup that sits better; products and peels absorb better afterwards. The hair grows back exactly as it was, not thicker or darker.","Who it's best for, and who should book something else: Dullness, peach fuzz, dry flaky skin, makeup that cakes, or as an add-on to a facial or peel. Not for severe active breakouts, open skin, very reactive skin or active cold sores."],"general":["A sterile blade gently removes the top layer of dead skin and the fine vellus hair (\"peach fuzz\"). Painless; skin is smooth and makeup sits better right away. Slight pinkness for a few hours is normal. Hair grows back the same (not thicker or darker; that's a myth), usually in three to five weeks. Commonly done every four to six weeks and often paired with a facial or a light peel, which then penetrates better. Not usually done on active breakouts, open skin or very reactive skin.","Skin is smooth and a little pink for a few hours. Makeup glides on the next day. No exfoliants for a few days; SPF daily, since the new surface burns easily. Hair returns as it was, in three to five weeks."],"shelf":[]},"5-1":{"evelyn":["What not to mix, or not on the same night: Not on the same night: a retinoid with an acid; two exfoliants; benzoyl peroxide with retinol. Vitamin C and Mandelic Acid goes in the morning, actives at night. Nothing active on skin that's freshly treated or waxed. Niacinamide and vitamin C are fine together."],"general":["Vitamin C (ascorbic acid and its derivatives) is an antioxidant that brightens, supports collagen and helps SPF do its job; used in the morning under sunscreen. Pure ascorbic acid can sting on a sensitized barrier.","Niacinamide, azelaic acid, tranexamic acid, kojic acid, licorice, arbutin, alpha-arbutin slow pigment production; they work gradually (8 to 12 weeks) and only hold with daily SPF.","Hydroquinone is a prescription-strength lightener in many places and a doctor's call."],"shelf":[]},"5-2":{"evelyn":["Retinal B3 Serum vs. Retinol Peptide Serum: Retinol is the gentler, slower one and where most people start; retinal works faster and is for skin that's already used to a retinoid or wants more. Either way: a pea for the whole face, 2 or 3 nights a week at first, moisturizer over it, SPF every morning, and never alongside an acid on the same night."],"general":["Retinol has to convert in the skin before it works; the gentlest, slowest, most widely used.","Retinal (retinaldehyde) is one step closer to the active form: faster results than retinol, a little more potential for irritation, still available without a prescription.","Prescription retinoids (tretinoin, adapalene, tazarotene) are stronger and are prescribed and managed by a doctor; they also change what an esthetician can safely do (see red flags and scope).","All retinoids: start low and slow (two or three nights a week), buff over moisturizer if sensitive, expect some dryness or flaking in the first weeks, use SPF daily, and pause before exfoliating treatments and waxing. Not used in pregnancy or while breastfeeding without a doctor's say-so; most estheticians treat that as a hard stop."],"shelf":[]},"5-3":{"evelyn":["What not to mix, or not on the same night: Not on the same night: a retinoid with an acid; two exfoliants; benzoyl peroxide with retinol. Vitamin C and Mandelic Acid goes in the morning, actives at night. Nothing active on skin that's freshly treated or waxed. Niacinamide and vitamin C are fine together."],"general":["Benzoyl peroxide kills acne bacteria and helps clear inflamed breakouts. It bleaches fabric (towels, pillowcases), can dry and irritate, and is usually started at a low strength (2.5% is often as effective as higher percentages with less irritation) for short contact or a few nights a week before building up.","Sulfur absorbs oil and calms inflamed spots; a common spot treatment and mask ingredient.","Salicylic acid and mandelic acid: see exfoliants.","Azelaic acid helps breakouts, redness and dark marks; often well tolerated.","Hydrocolloid patches draw fluid from a whitehead and stop picking; they do nothing for deep, closed bumps."],"shelf":[]},"6-1":{"evelyn":["The order of steps, morning and night: Morning: cleanser, toner, serum, eye gel, moisturizer, SPF. Night: cleanse (double cleanse if you wore makeup or SPF), toner, your treatment product (your active or acne med), serum, eye gel, moisturizer. Thin to thick, and a spot treatment goes on last, only on the spot. Please be sure to wash hands before applying eye products to prevent actives from bleeding into eye area and causing dryness or irritation."],"general":["Thin to thick, water-based before oil-based, treatment products on clean skin, sunscreen last in the morning.","Morning: cleanse (or just rinse, for dry skin) → toner or mist → serum (vitamin C, niacinamide, hydrating) → eye product → moisturizer → sunscreen.","Night: cleanse (double cleanse if wearing makeup or sunscreen) → toner → treatment (acid, retinoid, acne active) → serum → eye product → moisturizer. Spot treatments go on last, on the spot only.","A toner is optional; a hydrating one adds water, an exfoliating one counts as an active."],"shelf":[]},"6-2":{"evelyn":["What not to mix, or not on the same night: Not on the same night: a retinoid with an acid; two exfoliants; benzoyl peroxide with retinol. Vitamin C and Mandelic Acid goes in the morning, actives at night. Nothing active on skin that's freshly treated or waxed. Niacinamide and vitamin C are fine together."],"general":["A retinoid and an acid (AHA/BHA) the same night. Alternate nights instead.","Two exfoliants at once (two acids, an acid plus a scrub, an acid plus an exfoliating cleanser every day). Pick one.","Benzoyl peroxide and retinol together can cancel each other and irritate; most routines use benzoyl peroxide in the morning and the retinoid at night, or alternate.","Pure vitamin C and an acid or retinoid in the same step: usually vitamin C in the morning, actives at night.","Niacinamide and vitamin C are fine together; that caution is outdated."],"shelf":[]},"6-3":{"evelyn":["What do you ask a client first: What did you start, and when? Where are the bumps: the places you usually break out, or somewhere new? Do they sting or itch? How long have they been there?","How long until they see results: 4 to 6 weeks Purging shows up in the first 2 to 6 weeks after starting an active, in the spots you usually break out, and clears fast. A reaction shows up in new places, with redness, itching or stinging, and keeps going: stop and message me.","When must this come to you, or to a doctor: Bumps that are spreading to new areas, stinging, itchy or hive-like, or any purge that's still going after 6 to 8 weeks. Send me a photo and we adjust.","I broke out after my facial. Is that normal: If you are a part of my acne program and are on a new routine and starting acne treatments it is very normal to purge/breakout and the amount of time everyone purge varies. After normal custom facial a few small whiteheads a day or two after extractions is normal: what I loosened is surfacing. Don't pick, keep it simple and clean, and they settle within a few days. If it's spreading, painful or hot, send me a photo."],"general":["Purging happens in the first two to six weeks after starting something that speeds turnover (acids, retinoids, benzoyl peroxide, an acne program): clogs that were already forming surface faster, in the places the client usually breaks out, as small whiteheads or bumps that clear quickly. It settles within about six weeks. A breakout from a product shows up in new places, with itching, redness or stinging, lasts longer, and keeps going. Purging is a reason to keep going (while easing frequency if it's harsh); a reaction is a reason to stop and message Evelyn.","One new product at a time, two weeks apart, so a reaction has a clear cause.","Patch test behind the ear or on the jaw for two or three nights before using an active on the whole face.","Actives start two or three nights a week and build up as the skin allows. A new sting or flaking means slow down, not push through.","Expect the first few weeks of a retinoid or acid to include some dryness or small breakouts; judge a routine at 8 to 12 weeks, not 8 to 12 days."],"shelf":[]},"7-1":{"evelyn":["What can I do between visits to keep my results: Stick to the routine, SPF every day, hands off your face, clean pillowcases and phone screen, and don't start new products without checking with me. That's most of the result right there.","What happens at a first visit, from the moment they arrive: We start by talking: your skin, what you're using now, what you want. I look at your skin up close, then we go into a fully customized facial: double cleanse, enzyme exfoliation, extractions, a targeted mask, massage, LED and cryo globes. You leave with a home routine and a plan for the next visit."],"general":["Thin to thick, water-based before oil-based, treatment products on clean skin, sunscreen last in the morning.","Morning: cleanse (or just rinse, for dry skin) → toner or mist → serum (vitamin C, niacinamide, hydrating) → eye product → moisturizer → sunscreen.","Night: cleanse (double cleanse if wearing makeup or sunscreen) → toner → treatment (acid, retinoid, acne active) → serum → eye product → moisturizer. Spot treatments go on last, on the spot only.","A toner is optional; a hydrating one adds water, an exfoliating one counts as an active."],"shelf":[]},"7-2":{"evelyn":["What can I do between visits to keep my results: Stick to the routine, SPF every day, hands off your face, clean pillowcases and phone screen, and don't start new products without checking with me. That's most of the result right there."],"general":["Thin to thick, water-based before oil-based, treatment products on clean skin, sunscreen last in the morning.","Morning: cleanse (or just rinse, for dry skin) → toner or mist → serum (vitamin C, niacinamide, hydrating) → eye product → moisturizer → sunscreen.","Night: cleanse (double cleanse if wearing makeup or sunscreen) → toner → treatment (acid, retinoid, acne active) → serum → eye product → moisturizer. Spot treatments go on last, on the spot only.","A toner is optional; a hydrating one adds water, an exfoliating one counts as an active."],"shelf":[]},"7-3":{"evelyn":["I'm traveling. What should I bring: Cleanser, moisturizer, SPF and your main treatment product; leave the rest. Plane air is dry, so bring a hydrating mist or go heavier on moisturizer, and if you'll be in the sun, ease off the actives that week.","Anything you always say about sunscreen: Every morning, rain or shine, SPF 30 or higher, enough of it, and reapplied when you're outside. It's the one product that protects everything else we're doing: every active makes you burn more easily, and sun is what brings dark spots back."],"general":["Cleanser, moisturizer, sunscreen and the one treatment product the routine is built around; leave the rest. Plane air is dry, so a hydrating mist and more moisturizer help, and sun exposure on vacation is the usual reason to go easier on actives for a week.","UV is the main cause of early lines, uneven tone, dark spots and melasma, and it undoes pigment work. Daily broad-spectrum SPF 30 or higher is the one thing every esthetician agrees on. After any exfoliating treatment or active, skin burns more easily for days to weeks.","See skin basics. Dry skin wants richer creams and gentle cleansing; dehydrated skin wants humectants under a moisturizer, fewer foaming cleansers and acids, and a humidifier in dry rooms. Both want SPF."],"shelf":[]},"8-1":{"evelyn":["What do you ask a client first: What are your skin concerns and what does your current routine look like?","When must this come to you, or to a doctor: Acne that hasn't moved after months- a year of doing the recommendations and routine properly, breakouts that are clearly tied to your cycle, or anything that looks infected: that's when I bring in a dermatologist, and anything about medications is your doctor's."],"general":["Acne is pores clogged by oil and dead skin, with bacteria and inflammation on top. Non-inflamed acne is blackheads (open, oxidized) and whiteheads (closed); inflamed acne is red papules, pustules, and deeper nodules and cysts. Most acne routines combine: gentle cleansing twice a day, an exfoliant that keeps pores clear (mandelic or salicylic acid), an antibacterial (benzoyl peroxide, sulfur), hydration that doesn't clog (gel moisturizers), SPF, and nothing in the routine that feeds it (heavy oils, comedogenic makeup, hair products on the hairline, dirty pillowcases, phones). It takes 8 to 12 weeks to judge, and consistency beats intensity. Cystic, scarring or painful acne, acne that hasn't moved after months of consistent care, or acne that's clearly hormonal usually needs a doctor alongside the esthetician.","Oil production rises with androgens, which is why breakouts cluster at puberty, around periods, with some birth control changes, with PCOS and in stressful stretches. Jawline and chin breakouts that come and go monthly are classic. Routines help, but when the driver is hormonal, a doctor is often part of the answer. That's a hand-off, not a product.","Blackheads are oxidized oil in open pores; sebaceous filaments (the grey dots on the nose that come back in weeks) are normal and can only be minimized. Salicylic acid, retinoids, clay, and professional extractions clear congestion; pore strips and squeezing stretch pores and leave marks."],"shelf":[]},"8-2":{"evelyn":["Morning: what do you recommend from your shelf: For morning routine with dry/normal skin clients look like barrier cream cleanser, hydraremedy gel serum, soothing radiance toner, 5%-8% (depends on clients tolerance), cran peptide, eye gel and mineral SPF or brilliant defense For oily/combo/normal it usually looks like ultra gentle gel cleanser, moisture balance, eye gel, 8-15% mandelic (depends on tolerance), barrier gel cream or mineral SPF or brilliant Sometimes its mixed around really varies","Her picks: Barrier Balance Creamy Cleanser, Soothing Radiance Toner, 8% L-Mandelic Serum, 5% L-Mandelic Serum, 15% L-Mandelic Serum, 11% L-Mandelic Serum, Ultra Gentle Gel Cleanser, HydraRemedy Gel Serum and 8 more.","Her night routines: Night routines vary but usually it looks like this for oily/combo skin types it usually looks like ultra gentle cleanser, moisture balance toner, hydrabalance gel serum, 2.5% 5% acne med, barrier gel cream and for dry skin types barrier cream cleanser, hydraremedy gel serum, soothing radiance toner, cran peptide cream and if clients are on acne med and retinol/retinal they just alternate acne med and retinol at night","What should they avoid: Do not over exfoliate avoid using actives without being given aftercare instructions first, always follow aftercare instructions and if anything is burning, stinging or sensitive reach out immediately to make changes. If skin is experiencing Eczema,dermatitis, Psoriasis or any unusual reactions/flare ups stop actives immediately (list examples of active ingredients for them so they are aware of what to stop)"],"general":["Benzoyl peroxide kills acne bacteria and helps clear inflamed breakouts. It bleaches fabric (towels, pillowcases), can dry and irritate, and is usually started at a low strength (2.5% is often as effective as higher percentages with less irritation) for short contact or a few nights a week before building up.","Sulfur absorbs oil and calms inflamed spots; a common spot treatment and mask ingredient.","Salicylic acid and mandelic acid: see exfoliants.","Azelaic acid helps breakouts, redness and dark marks; often well tolerated.","Hydrocolloid patches draw fluid from a whitehead and stop picking; they do nothing for deep, closed bumps."],"shelf":[{"id":"fr-2-5pct-adv-acne-med","name":"2.5% Advanced Acne Med","brand":"Face Reality","price":29,"about":"Benzoyl peroxide acne medication."},{"id":"fr-5pct-adv-acne-med","name":"5% Advanced Acne Med","brand":"Face Reality","price":30,"about":"Benzoyl peroxide acne medication."},{"id":"fr-invisiclear","name":"invisiClear Hydrocolloid Spot Patches","brand":"Face Reality","price":10,"about":"Professional-grade patches that visibly flatten and drain breakouts. They cover and protect, they're translucent, and they suit all skin types."},{"id":"fr-sulfur-spot-treatment","name":"Sulfur Spot Treatment","brand":"Face Reality","price":35,"about":"A 6% sulfur acne treatment."},{"id":"mx-hydroxi","name":"Hydroxi","brand":"Mixi Skincare","price":40,"about":"Hydrogen peroxide acne cream."}]},"8-3":{"evelyn":["How long until they see results: 3 to 6 months I tell everyone give it 90 days to start seeing real results but it varies by severity of skin concerns, consistency, and proper home care and following it correctly","Habits and everyday tips you give: Habits & Everyday Skincare Tips — At Lumevina Aesthetics Spa, I educate clients on how their everyday habits can affect their skin, especially when treating acne, sensitivity, dehydration, and a compromised skin barrier. My approach focuses on consistency, proper hygiene, realistic lifestyle changes, and maintaining a personalized skincare routine. 1. Pillowcases & Bedding - Change pillowcases regularly, ideally 2–3 times per week, especially if you're acne-prone or use heavy hair products. - Keep bedding clean to minimize the buildup of oils, sweat, and product residue. - Avoid heavily fragranced detergents and fabric softeners if your skin is sensitive or easily irritated. - Clean towels should always be used when drying your face. Gently pat rather than rub. ### 8. Sunscreen & Sun Exposure - Daily SPF is one of my biggest non-negotiables. - Apply a generous amount of broad-spectrum SPF 30 or higher every morning. I generally recommend approximately two finger-lengths for the face and neck, adjusting as needed for adequate coverage. - Reapply every two hours while outdoors, and after swimming or sweating. - Sun protection is especially important for clients treating acne marks, hyperpigmentation, or undergoing chemical peels. - I remind clients that sunscreen is important even when it's cloudy. - Clients undergoing microchanneling or certain advanced treatments must follow their specific aftercare instructions, which may temporarily restrict sunscreen application immediately after treatment. ### 9. Skincare Consistency & Skin Barrier Health - Consistency is more important than using an excessive number of products. - Follow the personalized morning and evening routine provided by your esthetician. - Do not introduce multiple new active ingredients at once without guidance. - More exfoliation does not mean faster results. - Dryness, burning, persistent redness, and excessive peeling may indicate irritation or an impaired skin barrier. - If irritation develops, contact me before continuing or increasing active ingredients. - Barrier repair days may be incorporated into acne routines to allow the skin to recover. - Acne treatments such as mandelic acid and benzoyl peroxide must be introduced and adjusted according to skin tolerance. - Apply skincare in the recommended order, allowing products to absorb and dry as directed, particularly before applying stronger acne treatments. - Avoid applying strong actives around the eyes, eyelids, corners of the nose, and lips unless specifically instructed. - Never assume that an active breakout means your routine isn't working. Acne treatment requires time, consistency, and periodic adjustments. ### 10. Expectations & Professional Guidance - Healthy skin is a long-term commitment, not an overnight transformation. - Acne treatment often requires several months of consistency, depending on the type and severity of acne. - Professional treatments and homecare work together. One cannot completely replace the other. - Avoid constantly switching products based on social media trends. - Products that work for someone else may not be appropriate for your skin. - I encourage clients to communicate any changes, irritation, new medications, or concerns so their treatment plan can be adjusted safely. - My philosophy is to educate clients rather than overwhelm them with unnecessary products or unrealistic expectations. Important chatbot guideline: These are general educational recommendations, not universal rules. The AI should never assume every client needs the same products, diet changes, or routine. It should prioritize the client's personalized Lumevina Aesthetics Spa treatment plan and recommend contacting me directly for individualized advice, particularly regarding active ingredients, irritation, or post-treatment care.","Her picks: Phones & Touching Your Face, Regularly clean your phone screen, especially if it frequently touches your cheeks., Avoid touching your face unnecessarily or resting your face in your hands., Never pick, squeeze, or pop active breakouts. This increases the risk of inflammation, post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation, and scarring., Keep your hands clean before applying skincare products., Hair Products & Hair Hygiene, Be mindful of hair products containing heavy oils, waxes, and pomades, especially if you experience forehead, temple, or hairline breakouts., Keep hair away from your face when sleeping or exercising if products tend to transfer onto your skin. and 27 more.","When must this come to you, or to a doctor: Acne that hasn't moved after months- a year of doing the recommendations and routine properly, breakouts that are clearly tied to your cycle, or anything that looks infected: that's when I bring in a dermatologist, and anything about medications is your doctor's.","How long until my acne clears up: I tell everyone to give it 90 days to start seeing real results, and 3 to 6 months to be mostly clear. It depends on how severe it is, how consistent you are, and following the home care exactly. Some purging in the first few weeks is normal."],"general":["Deep, tender bumps along the jawline, chin and neck that flare before periods, with birth control changes, with PCOS or stress. Topicals help the surface (mandelic, benzoyl peroxide, spot treatments, consistent routine), but the driver is internal, so a doctor (dermatologist, OB-GYN or endocrinologist) is often part of clearing it. Supplements and medications for it are not the chat's to suggest.","The same acne, with sweat, friction and hair products added. Shower right after sweating, a salicylic or benzoyl peroxide body wash (left on a minute before rinsing), clean towels and sheets, no heavy conditioners running down the back, breathable fabrics. Body skin is tougher, so stronger exfoliants are tolerated, but still introduced slowly.","Supplements are outside an esthetician's lane. Whether to take, stop, mix or dose one is a question for the client's doctor or pharmacist, and for Evelyn to hear about, not for the chat to answer."],"shelf":[]},"9-1":{"evelyn":["What should they avoid: Picking, sun without SPF, Not using daily SPF properly, harsh scrubs, over-exfoliating, and lightening products from the internet. Dark marks fade with time, protection and gentle consistent care, not with force.","Habits and everyday tips you give: SPF every single morning and reapplied every 2-3 hours outside, a hat in strong sun, hands off breakouts, and patience: marks take months to fade, faster on skin that isn't being irritated.","When must this come to you, or to a doctor: A dark patch that's new, growing, uneven or changing, or a spot that bleeds or itches, needs a doctor, not a product. Large symmetrical patches on the cheeks or upper lip can be melasma, which we manage together with a doctor."],"general":["Flat brown or red marks left after a breakout or irritation (post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation or erythema). They fade on their own over months; faster with daily SPF, gentle exfoliation, brighteners (niacinamide, vitamin C, azelaic, tranexamic, arbutin, mandelic acid), and no picking. Deeper tones mark more easily and keep marks longer. Pitted or raised scars are texture, not pigment, and need collagen-building treatments.","Vitamin C (ascorbic acid and its derivatives) is an antioxidant that brightens, supports collagen and helps SPF do its job; used in the morning under sunscreen. Pure ascorbic acid can sting on a sensitized barrier.","Niacinamide, azelaic acid, tranexamic acid, kojic acid, licorice, arbutin, alpha-arbutin slow pigment production; they work gradually (8 to 12 weeks) and only hold with daily SPF.","Hydroquinone is a prescription-strength lightener in many places and a doctor's call.","Deeper skin tones (more melanin) are more likely to leave a dark mark after any irritation, breakout or over-aggressive treatment (post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation). Gentler acids such as mandelic and lactic, careful peel depth, no picking, and daily SPF matter even more. Lighter skin tends to go red rather than dark. Neither is \"better\"; the aftercare rules just weigh differently."],"shelf":[]},"9-2":{"evelyn":["What should they avoid: Picking, sun without SPF, Not using daily SPF properly, harsh scrubs, over-exfoliating, and lightening products from the internet. Dark marks fade with time, protection and gentle consistent care, not with force."],"general":["Vitamin C (ascorbic acid and its derivatives) is an antioxidant that brightens, supports collagen and helps SPF do its job; used in the morning under sunscreen. Pure ascorbic acid can sting on a sensitized barrier.","Niacinamide, azelaic acid, tranexamic acid, kojic acid, licorice, arbutin, alpha-arbutin slow pigment production; they work gradually (8 to 12 weeks) and only hold with daily SPF.","Hydroquinone is a prescription-strength lightener in many places and a doctor's call."],"shelf":[]},"9-3":{"evelyn":["Habits and everyday tips you give: SPF every single morning and reapplied every 2-3 hours outside, a hat in strong sun, hands off breakouts, and patience: marks take months to fade, faster on skin that isn't being irritated.","When must this come to you, or to a doctor: A dark patch that's new, growing, uneven or changing, or a spot that bleeds or itches, needs a doctor, not a product. Large symmetrical patches on the cheeks or upper lip can be melasma, which we manage together with a doctor."],"general":["Sun spots are small, defined brown spots from cumulative sun. Melasma is larger, symmetrical, blotchy patches (cheeks, upper lip, forehead) driven by hormones (pregnancy, birth control) and sun, and it worsens with heat as well as UV. Both need relentless SPF, hats, and brighteners; melasma is stubborn, comes back with sun or heat, and is usually managed together with a doctor; aggressive peels and heat-based treatments can make it worse. Any new or changing dark patch should be seen by a professional.","UV is the main cause of early lines, uneven tone, dark spots and melasma, and it undoes pigment work. Daily broad-spectrum SPF 30 or higher is the one thing every esthetician agrees on. After any exfoliating treatment or active, skin burns more easily for days to weeks.","Mineral (zinc oxide, titanium dioxide) sits on the skin and reflects and absorbs UV; usually the first choice for sensitive, acne-prone, post-treatment and pregnant clients. Can leave a cast on deeper tones; tinted versions help.","Chemical (avobenzone, octocrylene and others) absorb UV; lighter textures, more likely to sting after a treatment.","Either way: broad spectrum, SPF 30 or more, enough of it (about a quarter teaspoon for the face), reapplied every two hours in the sun. Makeup with SPF is a top-up, not the base."],"shelf":[]},"10-1":{"evelyn":["Anything you always say about sunscreen: Every morning, rain or shine, SPF 30 or higher, enough of it, and reapplied when you're outside. It's the one product that protects everything else we're doing: every active makes you burn more easily, and sun is what brings dark spots back.","Who it's best for, and who should book something else: Fine lines, loss of firmness, dullness, dehydration, or anyone who wants a glow before an event. Suitable for all skin types. Active breakouts are better served by the acne treatments first."],"general":["Lines come from sun, expression, dryness and collagen loss with age. Daily SPF prevents most of it; retinoids, peptides and vitamin C support collagen; hydration softens lines that are really dehydration; professional collagen-inducing treatments (microchanneling, peels, oxygen and tightening facials) build on that. Results are gradual, over months, and are kept with maintenance.","UV is the main cause of early lines, uneven tone, dark spots and melasma, and it undoes pigment work. Daily broad-spectrum SPF 30 or higher is the one thing every esthetician agrees on. After any exfoliating treatment or active, skin burns more easily for days to weeks.","Tiny, controlled micro-injuries in the skin trigger it to make new collagen and elastin, and let serums (often growth factors) absorb. Used for acne scars, texture, pores, fine lines and uneven tone. Microchanneling devices stamp straight down; microneedling pens drag and are generally deeper and more medical. Expect redness like a sunburn for a day or two, tightness, sometimes light flaking, and no makeup for about a day. Results build over weeks as collagen forms, and a series (commonly four to six sessions, four weeks apart) is where the change shows. Not done on active acne, open skin, cold sores, during Accutane, or on skin that scars with keloids without a doctor's OK."],"shelf":[]},"10-2":{"evelyn":["Retinal B3 Serum vs. Retinol Peptide Serum: Retinol is the gentler, slower one and where most people start; retinal works faster and is for skin that's already used to a retinoid or wants more. Either way: a pea for the whole face, 2 or 3 nights a week at first, moisturizer over it, SPF every morning, and never alongside an acid on the same night.","Anything you always say about sunscreen: Every morning, rain or shine, SPF 30 or higher, enough of it, and reapplied when you're outside. It's the one product that protects everything else we're doing: every active makes you burn more easily, and sun is what brings dark spots back."],"general":["Retinol has to convert in the skin before it works; the gentlest, slowest, most widely used.","Retinal (retinaldehyde) is one step closer to the active form: faster results than retinol, a little more potential for irritation, still available without a prescription.","Prescription retinoids (tretinoin, adapalene, tazarotene) are stronger and are prescribed and managed by a doctor; they also change what an esthetician can safely do (see red flags and scope).","All retinoids: start low and slow (two or three nights a week), buff over moisturizer if sensitive, expect some dryness or flaking in the first weeks, use SPF daily, and pause before exfoliating treatments and waxing. Not used in pregnancy or while breastfeeding without a doctor's say-so; most estheticians treat that as a hard stop.","Humectants (glycerin, hyaluronic acid, sodium PCA, aloe) pull water into the skin. They work best on damp skin with a moisturizer over them; on their own in dry air they can leave skin feeling tighter.","Emollients and occlusives (squalane, shea, oils, dimethicone, petrolatum) smooth and seal. Gel creams are lighter, for oily or acne-prone skin; creams and balms for dry.","Ceramides, cholesterol and fatty acids are the skin's own barrier lipids; good when skin is sensitized or over-exfoliated.","Niacinamide (vitamin B3) calms, helps oil balance and the barrier, and softens dark marks over time. Generally well tolerated and fine alongside most actives, including vitamin C (the \"don't mix them\" idea is outdated)."],"shelf":[]},"10-3":{"evelyn":["What happens, step by step: Double cleanse, exfoliation, extractions if needed, then the age-supporting part: oxygen therapy and a professional Tightening Protein Booster to lift, firm and brighten, with massage, LED and cryo globes. Add dermaplaning or a peel for more exfoliation and glow; I'll tell you whether one or both fit your skin.","Does it hurt? How does it feel: Cool, plumping and relaxing. The oxygen feels like a cool breeze; the booster is a warm, tightening mask. Nothing uncomfortable.","How they'll look after, and for how long: None. You leave glowing and can go straight to dinner; makeup the next morning. With a peel added, light flaking on day 2 or 3 is possible.","How often should they book it: Every 4 to 6 weeks Monthly is where it shines; before an event, book it 3 to 5 days ahead.","When will they see results, and how long do they last: Firmer, smoother, brighter skin the same day, lasting a week or two, and it builds with regular visits. For lasting firmness it pairs with a home routine (retinoid, peptides, SPF) and, for deeper change, ProCell.","Who it's best for, and who should book something else: Fine lines, loss of firmness, dullness, dehydration, or anyone who wants a glow before an event. Suitable for all skin types. Active breakouts are better served by the acne treatments first."],"general":["A cleanse (often double), exfoliation (enzyme or a gentle acid), steam or softening, extractions where needed, a mask aimed at the skin's condition, massage, and finishing products with SPF. Common additions: LED light, oxygen, high frequency, cold tools. A facial can't change skin in one visit; it clears, calms and resets, and the home routine does the work between visits.","How often: most estheticians suggest every four to six weeks (one skin cycle) for maintenance; every two to four weeks during an acne program or a corrective series.","Peels an esthetician performs are superficial: they work in the top layers to improve texture, tone, dark marks, fine lines and breakouts, and are repeated in a series (commonly three to six, two to four weeks apart). Deeper peels are medical.","A no-downtime peel (light, \"lunchtime\"): slight pinkness for an hour or two, maybe light flaking on day two or three, makeup the next day.","A medium-depth esthetic peel (stronger acids or layered): pink to red for a day or two, tightness, visible flaking or peeling on days two to five, and a week of gentle care. Not before a big event; plan a week.","Peels sting or feel warm while on the skin, usually for a few minutes, and are neutralized or self-neutralize. Some skin barely flakes and still benefits; peeling isn't the measure of a peel working.","Tiny, controlled micro-injuries in the skin trigger it to make new collagen and elastin, and let serums (often growth factors) absorb. Used for acne scars, texture, pores, fine lines and uneven tone. Microchanneling devices stamp straight down; microneedling pens drag and are generally deeper and more medical. Expect redness like a sunburn for a day or two, tightness, sometimes light flaking, and no makeup for about a day. Results build over weeks as collagen forms, and a series (commonly four to six sessions, four weeks apart) is where the change shows. Not done on active acne, open skin, cold sores, during Accutane, or on skin that scars with keloids without a doctor's OK.","Red light calms and supports collagen; blue light targets acne bacteria. Painless, no downtime, cumulative; a nice finisher."],"shelf":[]},"11-1":{"evelyn":["When should a client stop a product and message you: If anything is burning, stinging or feels sensitive, reach out to me right away so we can make changes. If your skin has eczema, dermatitis, psoriasis or any unusual reaction or flare-up, stop your actives immediately and message me."],"general":["Usually a thinned barrier: too many actives, harsh cleansing, hot water, fragrance, wind, sun. The fix is subtraction: a gentle fragrance-free cleanser, a moisturizer with barrier lipids, mineral SPF, and actives paused and reintroduced one at a time. Persistent flushing with bumps across the cheeks and nose, or redness that comes with heat, alcohol or spicy food, can be rosacea, which is a doctor's diagnosis.","Common triggers for stinging and redness on sensitized skin. \"Unscented\" can still contain masking fragrance; \"fragrance-free\" is the clearer label.","Fragrance, essential oils, nuts (in some oils), latex (gloves, some wax strips), lanolin, certain preservatives, and sulfites. A client who has reacted before is patch-tested or given a different product. Any new rash is a reason to stop the product and message Evelyn."],"shelf":[]},"11-2":{"evelyn":["Who has to wait, or can't have it: Accutane in the last 6 to 12 months, a prescription retinoid in the last week, a sunburn or tan, active cold sores or breakouts, a recent laser or peel, pregnancy or breastfeeding, a named skin condition, or a keloid history: tell me first; several of these need your doctor's OK. Clients with Fitzpatrick skin types IV–VI, or anyone prone to hyperpigmentation, typically require 4–6 weeks of skin preparation before more intensive chemical peels. This may include tyrosinase inhibitors to help prevent pigmentation, daily broad-spectrum SPF 50, and a consistent skincare routine to maintain a healthy skin barrier. Preparation is customized based on skin type, pigmentation history, and peel strength to minimize the risk of post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation (PIH). Reach out before hand.","Who has to wait, or can't have it: If you're on Accutane or finished it in the last 6 to 12 months, have active breakouts or cold sores, are pregnant, scar with keloids, or take blood thinners, tell me first: some of those wait, some need your doctor's OK. Pause retinoids and acids for a week before."],"general":["These are the usual reasons an esthetician postpones or adapts a treatment. Each is a hand-off: the client's doctor and Evelyn decide, not the chat.","Accutane (isotretinoin): skin is fragile and slow to heal during and after. Waxing, peels, dermaplaning and microchanneling generally wait six to twelve months after the last dose, with the doctor's OK. Gentle, hydrating facials are often fine.","Prescription retinoids (tretinoin, adapalene, tazarotene) and strong acids: usually paused five to seven days before waxing, peels, dermaplaning and microchanneling, and for about a week after. Over-the-counter retinol and acids, three to five days.","Pregnancy and breastfeeding: many estheticians avoid retinoids, high-strength salicylic acid, hydroquinone, some peels, and electrical or heat-based modalities, and switch to gentle, pregnancy-safe facials; sensitivity and melasma risk are higher. The client's doctor has the final word.","Recent Botox or fillers: facials, massage and pressure on the area usually wait about two weeks; peels and dermaplaning, one to two weeks, per the injector."],"shelf":[]},"11-3":{"evelyn":["The assistant must never answer these. It always hands them to you.: Anything about supplements, and anything where someone asks what's wrong with their skin from a photo.","Her picks: Pregnancy or breastfeeding, Accutane, past or present, Prescription retinoids or antibiotics, Any other prescription, A reaction, rash, burning or swelling, Eczema, rosacea, psoriasis or another condition, Cold sores, Clients under 18 and 2 more.","Anything it should never say or promise: Never promise results or say something is \"guaranteed\" or \"cures\" anything. Never say what someone's skin condition is, never say a reaction is \"normal\", never recommend another brand or store, and never quote a price or policy that isn't on the website."],"general":["Trouble breathing, or swelling of the lips, tongue, throat or around the eyes: emergency, 911.","A reaction that spreads, blisters, weeps, hives, or gets worse after the first day.","Signs of infection: increasing pain, heat, pus, red streaks, fever.","A mole or spot that is new, changing, asymmetrical, with an irregular border, more than one color, larger than a pencil eraser, or that bleeds or itches (the ABCDEs).","Cystic, scarring or painful acne; acne that hasn't responded to months of consistent care; suspected hormonal acne.","A licensed esthetician works on the surface of the skin to cleanse, exfoliate, improve and beautify: facials, superficial peels, dermaplaning, extractions, waxing, LED, skincare guidance and a home routine. An esthetician does not diagnose skin disease, treat medical conditions, prescribe or advise on medication, perform invasive or medical procedures, or promise results. When something looks medical, the answer is a referral to a doctor (a dermatologist for skin, the client's own doctor for medications and health). State boards set the exact limits; Evelyn knows hers."],"shelf":[]},"12-1":{"evelyn":["Do I need to stop my products before my facial? Which ones, and for how long: Yes please. Pause retinol or any retinoid, acids (glycolic, lactic, salicylic, mandelic), scrubs and at-home peels for 3 to 5 days before your facial; a prescription retinoid like tretinoin, about a week, and tell me you're on it. Keep your cleanser, moisturizer and SPF as usual. Be sure to at least apply moisturizer and SPF the day of your appointment.","I'm going on vacation / I just got some sun. Can I still get my treatment: No treatment on sunburned or freshly tanned skin, and no sun or tanning for a day or two after a wax, peel or dermaplaning. For a trip, book at least 2 or 3 days before you leave, and I'll go gentler on the actives if you're going to be in the sun.","I'm breaking out right now. Should I still come in: Please do. Breakouts are exactly what a treatment is for: I'll adjust what we do to calm them and clear what's ready. The only thing I won't work on is open, raw or infected skin, so if anything is weeping or very painful, send me a photo first.","Anything to do before this one, beyond your general list: Pause retinoids and acids for a week, no waxing or dermaplaning on the face for a week, no sun, and plan the week after to be a quiet one for your skin.","Anything to do before this one, beyond your general list: No retinoids, acids, scrubs or dermaplaning for a week before, no sun or tanning, no waxing on the face that week, and come with clean skin and no makeup.","Can I still come in if I'm on my period: Yes, you can come in. Skin can be a little more sensitive around your period, so a facial may feel more reactive and a wax a bit more tender, but nothing about it is off limits. For a Brazilian, a tampon or cup is fine."],"general":["These are the usual reasons an esthetician postpones or adapts a treatment. Each is a hand-off: the client's doctor and Evelyn decide, not the chat.","Accutane (isotretinoin): skin is fragile and slow to heal during and after. Waxing, peels, dermaplaning and microchanneling generally wait six to twelve months after the last dose, with the doctor's OK. Gentle, hydrating facials are often fine.","Prescription retinoids (tretinoin, adapalene, tazarotene) and strong acids: usually paused five to seven days before waxing, peels, dermaplaning and microchanneling, and for about a week after. Over-the-counter retinol and acids, three to five days.","Pregnancy and breastfeeding: many estheticians avoid retinoids, high-strength salicylic acid, hydroquinone, some peels, and electrical or heat-based modalities, and switch to gentle, pregnancy-safe facials; sensitivity and melasma risk are higher. The client's doctor has the final word.","Recent Botox or fillers: facials, massage and pressure on the area usually wait about two weeks; peels and dermaplaning, one to two weeks, per the injector."],"shelf":[]},"12-2":{"evelyn":["The first 24 hours: Nothing on your skin for the rest of the day: no makeup, no touching or picking. No workouts, sauna, steam, hot yoga, hot showers on your face or swimming for 24 hours. Tonight or tomorrow morning, a gentle cleanse, moisturizer and SPF.","The first week: Keep it simple: gentle cleanser, hydration, moisturizer, SPF every morning. No acids, retinol, scrubs or new products for 2 or 3 days. If it's a chemical peel or microchanneling treatment wait 7 days to introduce actives. Fresh pillowcase, clean phone screen, and let anything from extractions settle on its own.","What's normal: A little pink or flushed for a few hours, slight tightness, mild tenderness where I did extractions, and maybe a few small whiteheads surfacing in the next day or two. All of that settles on its own.","What isn't normal: Redness that spreads or gets worse after the first day, swelling (especially around the eyes or lips), hives, blistering, burning that doesn't settle, or a spot that's hot, painful or full of pus. Message me with a photo, and for anything spreading fast or any trouble breathing, see a doctor or call 911.","The first 24 hours: No makeup today, no heat, sweat, sun or swimming for 24 to 48 hours, no touching. Tonight or tomorrow, a gentle cleanse, moisturizer and SPF. Nothing exfoliating.","The first week: Gentle cleanser, moisturizer, SPF every morning, hat if you're out. No acids, retinol, scrubs or waxing for about a week. If you flake, let it come off by itself."],"general":["The first 24 hours - Leave the skin alone: no makeup the rest of the day (mineral makeup is often allowed the next morning), no touching or picking. - No heat or sweat: skip workouts, saunas, steam rooms, hot yoga, hot showers on the face and swimming (chlorine and salt) for 24 to 48 hours. Sweat and heat on freshly exfoliated skin cause stinging and breakouts. - Cleanse gently that night or the next morning with a mild cleanser and lukewarm water; moisturize; SPF in the morning. - No actives: acids, retinoids, scrubs, benzoyl peroxide and exfoliating toners stay off for at least two or three days after a facial, five to seven days after a peel, dermaplaning or microchanneling, or until the skin has stopped flaking and feels normal. - Sun: avoid direct sun and tanning for a week, SPF every morning, reapplied outdoors.","The first week - Hydrate and protect: gentle cleanser, hydrating serum or mist, moisturizer, SPF. - Let flaking fall on its own. Peeling skin that's pulled off leaves dark marks or raw patches. - Change pillowcases and clean phone screens, especially after extractions. - Hold off on waxing, threading, dermaplaning at home, facial scrubs and new products for a week.","What's normal - Pink or slightly flushed skin for a few hours (longer after a peel). - Tightness and dryness for a day or two. - Light flaking on days two to five after a peel, dermaplaning or microchanneling. - A few small whiteheads or bumps in the day or two after extractions, as what was loosened surfaces; a short \"purge\" in acne-prone skin after a peel. - Mild tenderness where extractions were done.","What isn't normal, and means message Evelyn or see a doctor - Redness that spreads or gets worse after the first day instead of better. - Swelling, especially around the eyes or lips; hives; blistering; weeping or crusting. - Burning that doesn't settle in a day, or pain. - Pus, heat and spreading redness around a spot (possible infection), or fever. - A cold sore starting (lip tingling or blisters): a doctor can prescribe something fast. For breathing trouble or swelling of the lips, tongue or throat, call 911.","Peeling, if it comes, usually starts on day two or three and finishes by day five to seven. Don't pick or exfoliate it off. Keep the skin moisturized and out of the sun; a hat helps. Skin can look darker or blotchy before it flakes; that's the old surface lifting, not the result.","Red like a sunburn for 12 to 48 hours, warm and tight, sometimes pinpoint marks or light flaking by day three. Only the products given at the visit for the first day or two; no makeup for 24 hours; no sweat, heat or sun for 48 hours; no actives for about a week. Collagen builds for weeks, so the real result shows between sessions."],"shelf":[]},"12-3":{"evelyn":["What's normal: A little pink or flushed for a few hours, slight tightness, mild tenderness where I did extractions, and maybe a few small whiteheads surfacing in the next day or two. All of that settles on its own.","What isn't normal: Redness that spreads or gets worse after the first day, swelling (especially around the eyes or lips), hives, blistering, burning that doesn't settle, or a spot that's hot, painful or full of pus. Message me with a photo, and for anything spreading fast or any trouble breathing, see a doctor or call 911.","What's normal: Pink to red for a day or two, tightness, and darker or blotchy-looking skin before it flakes; that's the old surface lifting, not the result. Visible peeling on days 2 to 5.","What isn't normal: Blistering, weeping or crusting, swelling, hives, redness that spreads or gets worse after day two, severe pain, or a cold sore starting. Message me with a photo right away; some of those need a doctor the same day."],"general":["Exfoliated, extracted or waxed skin has a thinner barrier and open follicles for a day or two. Heat, sweat, friction, bacteria (hands, phones, pillowcases, gym equipment) and strong actives in that window are what turn a good treatment into redness, bumps or dark marks.","Trouble breathing, or swelling of the lips, tongue, throat or around the eyes: emergency, 911.","A reaction that spreads, blisters, weeps, hives, or gets worse after the first day.","Signs of infection: increasing pain, heat, pus, red streaks, fever.","A mole or spot that is new, changing, asymmetrical, with an irregular border, more than one color, larger than a pencil eraser, or that bleeds or itches (the ABCDEs).","Cystic, scarring or painful acne; acne that hasn't responded to months of consistent care; suspected hormonal acne."],"shelf":[]},"13-1":{"evelyn":["How long does my hair need to be for a wax: About a quarter inch, the length of a grain of rice: roughly 2 to 3 weeks after shaving, or 4 to 6 weeks after your last wax. Too short and the wax can't grip it.","Should I shave or trim before my wax: No shaving, please; it resets the growth and makes the next wax patchier and more uncomfortable. If it's very long you can trim, but not shorter than a quarter inch. Exfoliate gently 2 or 3 days before and come with clean, dry skin and no lotion.","It's my first Brazilian. Any tips: Let the hair grow to a quarter inch, exfoliate a couple of days before, shower that day, and wear loose cotton. It takes about 30 to 40 minutes and the first one is the most sensitive; it gets easier every time. Tell me first about any medications, skin issues or pregnancy.","What happens, step by step: You undress from the waist down and lie on the table; I cleanse and prep the skin, apply warm hard wax in sections and remove it without strips, working front to back, then tweeze any strays and finish with a soothing product. The mini vajacial adds extractions, ingrown removal and a cooling mask.","Does it hurt? How does it feel: A quick sting as each section comes off, then it fades. Hard wax is gentler than strip wax. Breathe out as I pull, and it's over in 30 to 40 minutes. The first is the most sensitive.","How they'll look after, and for how long: Red, warm and a little tender for a few hours; small bumps for up to a day. Loose clothes, no heat, sweat or sex for 24 hours, and you're fine."],"general":["Hair length: about a quarter inch (6 mm), the length of a grain of rice: roughly two to three weeks of growth after shaving, or four to six weeks after a wax. Too short and the wax can't grip; very long can be trimmed, but not shorter than that.","Don't shave between waxes. Shaving resets the growth cycle and makes the next wax patchier and more painful. Waxing gets easier with regular visits because the hair comes in finer and sparser.","Exfoliate gently two or three days before (not the day of), and moisturize in the days before so the skin isn't dry or tight.","Day of: clean, dry skin; no lotion, oil or deodorant on the area; loose, breathable clothing; some clients take an over-the-counter pain reliever 30 to 45 minutes before (their call, and their doctor's if they take other medicines).","Periods: waxing is possible during a period (a tampon or cup for a Brazilian); skin is often more sensitive in the days before and during, so some clients prefer to book around it.","Let the hair grow to the right length, exfoliate a couple of days before, shower that day, wear loose cotton, breathe out as each strip comes off, and know the first one is the most sensitive; it gets easier each time. It takes about 30 to 40 minutes. Tell the esthetician about any medications, skin issues or pregnancy first (pregnancy waxing is common but the skin is more sensitive; some clients clear it with their doctor)."],"shelf":[]},"13-2":{"evelyn":["I have little bumps after my wax. What should I do: Small red bumps for a few hours to a day are normal; it's the follicles reacting. Keep the area clean, cool and loose, no heat, sweat or tight clothes for a day or two, then exfoliate gently a few times a week to keep ingrowns away. If bumps turn into pus-filled spots or spread, message me right away.","How often should I get waxed? How long does it last: Every 4 to 6 weeks for most areas; brows, lip and underarms often every 3 to 4. You'll be smooth for 2 to 3 weeks, and it lasts longer the more regular you are, because the hair grows back finer and sparser.","Does waxing hurt less over time: Yes, as long as you don't shave in between. The hair comes in finer and thinner each time, so every visit is quicker and less uncomfortable than the one before. The first is the hardest.","The first 24 hours: For 24 to 48 hours: no heat or sweat (workouts, hot showers, saunas, hot tubs, pools), no sun or tanning, no tight clothes on the area, no scented lotions, oils or deodorant. A soothing, fragrance-free product or aloe is fine.","The first week: From day 2 or 3, exfoliate the area gently 2 or 3 times a week and keep it moisturized; that's what prevents ingrowns. No shaving between waxes. Book your next one for 4 to 6 weeks out.","What's normal: Redness, warmth and small red bumps for a few hours up to a day, longer on a first wax or sensitive skin, and a few tiny white bumps a day or two later as the follicles settle."],"general":["No heat or sweat: workouts, hot showers, baths, saunas, hot tubs, swimming.","No sun or tanning on the area.","No tight clothing on the waxed area; for a Brazilian, loose cotton underwear and no sex for about 24 hours.","No touching, no scented lotions, oils or deodorant on the area for the first day; a soothing, fragrance-free product or aloe is fine.","No exfoliating for 48 to 72 hours.","Starting two or three days after, exfoliate the area gently two or three times a week (a soft mitt or a gentle acid toner or pad; mandelic, lactic, glycolic or salicylic are common) and keep it moisturized. Ingrowns come from dead skin trapping new hair; exfoliation and no tight friction are the fix. Don't dig at an ingrown with tweezers; a warm compress and exfoliation let it surface, and a stubborn, painful or pus-filled one is a reason to message Evelyn or see a doctor.","Redness, warmth and small red bumps (a histamine response around the follicles) for a few hours to a day, longer for the first wax or on sensitive skin.","A few tiny white bumps a day or two later as follicles settle."],"shelf":[]},"14-1":{"evelyn":["I don't know which facial to book. Which one is for me: If you're new and don't have acne concerns, book New Clients Start Here. If you're new with acne concerns, book the Acne Bootcamp consultation + treatment. Returning clients: the Lumevina Custom Facial, or the Monthly Acne Treatment if we're working on acne. Not sure? Message me and I'll point you to the right one.","What happens at a first visit, from the moment they arrive: We start by talking: your skin, what you're using now, what you want. I look at your skin up close, then we go into a fully customized facial: double cleanse, enzyme exfoliation, extractions, a targeted mask, massage, LED and cryo globes. You leave with a home routine and a plan for the next visit.","What should they wear or bring: Just yourself. Come with a clean face if you can (I'll remove makeup if not), apply moisturizer and SPF day before appointment, hair you don't mind getting a little damp at the hairline, and for a wax, loose comfortable clothing. Bring a list or photos of what you're using at home.","Can I add something on the day, like dermaplaning: Usually, there is always time for add ons. If it is microchanneling please let me know at least one day in advance.","Can I switch to a different treatment when I get there: Often yes, if the time allows and your skin is right for it; just tell me when you arrive or message me beforehand so I can plan. Switching to something longer isn't always possible on the day.","Can they arrive wearing makeup: Yes, I'll remove it Bare-faced saves a little time, but don't worry about it; a double cleanse is the first step of every facial."],"general":["A look at the skin, a review of the current routine and habits, and a plan: which treatment, which products and in what order. Virtual consultations work for routine questions; in-person is better when the skin needs to be seen up close.","Clearing acne is a process, not a facial: a treatment every two to four weeks (exfoliation, extractions, calming and antibacterial steps, often a peel or LED) plus a strict, slowly escalated home routine. Most programs count on 90 days to see a real difference and three to six months to be mostly clear, with consistency at home as the deciding factor. Purging in the first weeks is common.","A cleanse (often double), exfoliation (enzyme or a gentle acid), steam or softening, extractions where needed, a mask aimed at the skin's condition, massage, and finishing products with SPF. Common additions: LED light, oxygen, high frequency, cold tools. A facial can't change skin in one visit; it clears, calms and resets, and the home routine does the work between visits.","How often: most estheticians suggest every four to six weeks (one skin cycle) for maintenance; every two to four weeks during an acne program or a corrective series.","These are the usual reasons an esthetician postpones or adapts a treatment. Each is a hand-off: the client's doctor and Evelyn decide, not the chat.","Accutane (isotretinoin): skin is fragile and slow to heal during and after. Waxing, peels, dermaplaning and microchanneling generally wait six to twelve months after the last dose, with the doctor's OK. Gentle, hydrating facials are often fine.","Prescription retinoids (tretinoin, adapalene, tazarotene) and strong acids: usually paused five to seven days before waxing, peels, dermaplaning and microchanneling, and for about a week after. Over-the-counter retinol and acids, three to five days.","Pregnancy and breastfeeding: many estheticians avoid retinoids, high-strength salicylic acid, hydroquinone, some peels, and electrical or heat-based modalities, and switch to gentle, pregnancy-safe facials; sensitivity and melasma risk are higher. The client's doctor has the final word."],"shelf":[]},"14-2":{"evelyn":["Do I need everything you recommended, or what should I start with: If you're working with a budget, we can absolutely start with the essentials: cleanser, hydrator, moisturizer, and SPF**, then gradually introduce additional products as you're ready. Just keep in mind that this approach may slow down your progress since we won't be addressing all of your skin concerns right away. However, if you're enrolled in my Acne Program, I highly recommend starting with your complete recommended routine, including your treatment products (actives). These play an important role in targeting acne, preventing new breakouts, and helping us achieve consistent results. My goal is always to create a routine that's both effective and realistic for you, so we can discuss your priorities and budget together!","The order of steps, morning and night: Morning: cleanser, toner, serum, eye gel, moisturizer, SPF. Night: cleanse (double cleanse if you wore makeup or SPF), toner, your treatment product (your active or acne med), serum, eye gel, moisturizer. Thin to thick, and a spot treatment goes on last, only on the spot. Please be sure to wash hands before applying eye products to prevent actives from bleeding into eye area and causing dryness or irritation.","Starting the mandelic serums: Start with the strength I give you, usually the lowest, a few nights a week, and build up to nightly as your skin allows. Moving up a strength is my call, after a few weeks with no stinging or flaking. If it ever burns or your skin gets sensitive, back off and message me.","Morning: what do you recommend from your shelf: For morning routine with dry/normal skin clients look like barrier cream cleanser, hydraremedy gel serum, soothing radiance toner, 5%-8% (depends on clients tolerance), cran peptide, eye gel and mineral SPF or brilliant defense For oily/combo/normal it usually looks like ultra gentle gel cleanser, moisture balance, eye gel, 8-15% mandelic (depends on tolerance), barrier gel cream or mineral SPF or brilliant Sometimes its mixed around really varies","Her picks: Barrier Balance Creamy Cleanser, Soothing Radiance Toner, 8% L-Mandelic Serum, 5% L-Mandelic Serum, 15% L-Mandelic Serum, 11% L-Mandelic Serum, Ultra Gentle Gel Cleanser, HydraRemedy Gel Serum and 8 more.","Her night routines: Night routines vary but usually it looks like this for oily/combo skin types it usually looks like ultra gentle cleanser, moisture balance toner, hydrabalance gel serum, 2.5% 5% acne med, barrier gel cream and for dry skin types barrier cream cleanser, hydraremedy gel serum, soothing radiance toner, cran peptide cream and if clients are on acne med and retinol/retinal they just alternate acne med and retinol at night"],"general":["Thin to thick, water-based before oil-based, treatment products on clean skin, sunscreen last in the morning.","Morning: cleanse (or just rinse, for dry skin) → toner or mist → serum (vitamin C, niacinamide, hydrating) → eye product → moisturizer → sunscreen.","Night: cleanse (double cleanse if wearing makeup or sunscreen) → toner → treatment (acid, retinoid, acne active) → serum → eye product → moisturizer. Spot treatments go on last, on the spot only.","A toner is optional; a hydrating one adds water, an exfoliating one counts as an active."],"shelf":[{"id":"fr-11pct-mandelic-serum","name":"11% L-Mandelic Serum","brand":"Face Reality","price":46,"about":"Exfoliates and brightens."},{"id":"fr-15pct-mandelic-serum","name":"15% L-Mandelic Serum","brand":"Face Reality","price":48,"about":"Exfoliates and brightens."},{"id":"fr-5pct-mandelic-serum","name":"5% L-Mandelic Serum","brand":"Face Reality","price":42,"about":"Exfoliates and brightens."},{"id":"fr-8pct-mandelic-serum","name":"8% L-Mandelic Serum","brand":"Face Reality","price":44,"about":"Exfoliates and brightens."},{"id":"fr-barrier-cream-cleanser","name":"Barrier Balance Creamy Cleanser","brand":"Face Reality","price":37,"about":"A daily calming wash. Acne-safe."},{"id":"fr-barrier-gel-cream","name":"Barrier Care Gel Cream","brand":"Face Reality","price":38,"about":"Repairs, moisturizes and comforts, with Multi-Restore Complex. Acne-safe."}]}};
